RAVI CHINTALAPUDI:
Big hello and welcome,

00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:10.800
everyone to the Session CMP 103.

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:14.830
This session is about how do you
manage large fleets of Google

00:00:14.830 --> 00:00:17.210
Cloud compute engine VMs.

00:00:17.210 --> 00:00:19.470
And I'm Ravi Chintalapudi,
a product manager

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:20.640
in Google Cloud.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:24.000
And today, I'll walk you
through a few solutions what

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:26.850
we are building to address all
of our customer challenges,

00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:28.590
what we have heard
from you all, when

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:31.860
managing large fleets
of compute engine VMs.

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:33.340
I'll start with
the very high level

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:34.798
all of the different
problems, what

00:00:34.798 --> 00:00:36.350
we have heard from you all--

00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:38.243
an aggregated way,
summarized way there.

00:00:38.243 --> 00:00:39.660
And then we'll
talk about what are

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:43.890
the solutions we are building
to go address those challenges.

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:45.810
In that, specifically,
I'll drill down

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:49.550
on patch management,
configuration management.

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:52.230
I'll also give you a
background on why we chose this

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:55.020
and how we're going to go
progress and make our roadmap.

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:56.700
Going with demos--
deep dive into demos

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:59.730
and wrapping up with a roadmap.

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:02.880
I understand you'll be
listening to a lot of sessions

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:04.615
in Google [INAUDIBLE] next.

00:01:04.615 --> 00:01:06.220
So there'll be a lot of data.

00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:08.260
It's very hard to
remember everything.

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:09.770
But there is one key takeaway.

00:01:09.770 --> 00:01:12.840
If there's one thing I want you
to remember from this session,

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:14.250
that is this.

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:18.270
GCP now provides automated
patch management service,

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:21.900
configuration management service
across Windows and Linux,

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:24.480
across your Linux
distros, across

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:25.740
your hybrid environments.

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:27.860
Obviously, we
prioritize GCP first,

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:30.392
but things to come up
in the near future.

00:01:30.392 --> 00:01:34.240
With that, I'll get started.

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:35.915
So as I said, I'll
start with what

00:01:35.915 --> 00:01:38.040
are the top challenges,
what we have heard from you

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:41.760
all, when managing
your large VM fleet.

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:44.920
Then we'll talking about
how are we addressing that.

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:47.420
In the interest of time, I'll
talk about only a few of them.

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:49.550
The first one I'll start with--

00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:52.100
as more and more
customers starts

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:56.300
migrating their applications,
building their applications

00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:59.060
in GCP, they're often
dealing with thousands

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:03.300
of applications, 10 thousands
of VMs, petabytes of storage.

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:06.260
So managing this large
infrastructure at scale

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:08.449
easily and
effectively has become

00:02:08.449 --> 00:02:11.800
a big burden for our customers.

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:15.400
Second thing is most
of our customers

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:17.340
are used to some
management tools

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:20.862
when managing their
on-prem tools,

00:02:20.862 --> 00:02:22.330
their on-prem infrastructure.

00:02:22.330 --> 00:02:25.430
So as they make the
journey to the cloud,

00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:28.180
they expect the cloud
providers, like GCP,

00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:29.950
to provide some
native management

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:34.030
tools because either those
existing tools are not

00:02:34.030 --> 00:02:36.220
compatible with the one
that's running in the cloud,

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:40.100
or they might be working
with only specific cloud.

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:41.630
Third big problem
what we have seen

00:02:41.630 --> 00:02:44.270
is mostly from a
security standpoint.

00:02:44.270 --> 00:02:45.770
Think of a scenarios
where we had

00:02:45.770 --> 00:02:48.350
Wannacry, Meltdown, Spectre.

00:02:48.350 --> 00:02:51.540
When in any of these
vulnerabilities hit,

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:53.465
the biggest thing on
an IT admin's mind

00:02:53.465 --> 00:02:55.460
is, is my environment compliant?

00:02:55.460 --> 00:02:56.450
How bad is that?

00:02:56.450 --> 00:02:58.580
How can I bring it back
to the compliant state?

00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:02.520
Are all my VMs in a
desired configuration?

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:04.980
Which of them drifted, why
it drifted, how it drifted.

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:05.770
It's a lot.

00:03:05.770 --> 00:03:10.100
So managing security at a
scale is another big problem.

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:11.823
Well, these are just
some of the problems

00:03:11.823 --> 00:03:14.240
talking of what we have heard
from a lot of our customers.

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:15.740
In the interest of
time, I picked up

00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:17.070
the top thing what we heard.

00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:20.670
So what did really do after
hearing all these problems?

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:24.180
So we started with building a
suite of VM management tools.

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:26.360
In that, we prioritized
patch and config,

00:03:26.360 --> 00:03:29.840
keeping security and
compliance as the top view--

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:31.175
top priority for us.

00:03:31.175 --> 00:03:33.050
So over the next 15
minutes, 20 minutes, I'll

00:03:33.050 --> 00:03:36.038
walk you through each of the
solutions, and how we build it,

00:03:36.038 --> 00:03:38.705
and how can you use, how can you
observe it, and with the demos.

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:44.570
I'll start with
patch management.

00:03:44.570 --> 00:03:47.165
Patching servers is
not something new.

00:03:47.165 --> 00:03:49.040
Many of our customers
tell you, oh, no, we've

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:51.170
been patching servers
for years and years.

00:03:51.170 --> 00:03:55.130
But still it is one of the
top IT management problems.

00:03:55.130 --> 00:03:58.110
So we're really curious to know
why it was such a big pain.

00:03:58.110 --> 00:04:01.250
So we started talking to
about 100+ customers across

00:04:01.250 --> 00:04:04.820
the globe, across
the industries.

00:04:04.820 --> 00:04:07.520
Average size was ranging
from 500 to 20,000,

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:09.810
showing that we have
covered SMBs and even

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:11.240
the enterprise thing there.

00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:13.360
And here's a quick summary.

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:15.180
First thing is, a
patch Tuesday comes.

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:17.220
We know it happens on the
second Tuesday of every month.

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:19.637
The biggest thing on IT admin's
mind, especially the patch

00:04:19.637 --> 00:04:22.460
admin mind is what's the
state of my compliance, how

00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:23.610
compliant I am, how bad.

00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:24.110
I am.

00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:27.590
And this thing I want is a
report across my environment,

00:04:27.590 --> 00:04:29.780
across my feet,
whether I'm running

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:31.820
Windows or Linux or
different Linux distros,

00:04:31.820 --> 00:04:34.680
I want one report unified
across my environment,

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:36.650
so I can see the state.

00:04:36.650 --> 00:04:38.120
Second big thing
what we have heard

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:41.940
is reliability of patches
what does this really mean?

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:44.280
As I was talking to
many of the customers,

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:45.750
one thing is super clear.

00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:49.170
Most of them are patching
in a phased rollout.

00:04:49.170 --> 00:04:51.960
So they always start with
their dev environment first.

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:53.510
That goes fine.

00:04:53.510 --> 00:04:56.190
They go to staging,
then pro-prod to prod.

00:04:56.190 --> 00:04:58.690
They do this because
they're worried

00:04:58.690 --> 00:05:00.870
that a particular
patch or a package

00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:03.240
might create some
incompatibilities,

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:05.350
might break their
existing applications.

00:05:05.350 --> 00:05:08.040
So they're trying to hope
to find any of the issues

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:10.800
throughout this phased rollout,
so that they'll find something,

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:12.258
and they can fix
it before applying

00:05:12.258 --> 00:05:14.430
to the next environment.

00:05:14.430 --> 00:05:16.620
Third big category what
we have seen in general

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:18.780
orchestrated patching--
what does this really mean?

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:24.852
Most of our admins said they
spend nights and weekends only

00:05:24.852 --> 00:05:26.560
when they're doing
patching applications,

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:28.280
primarily when
patching applications.

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:30.697
Their reason for that is they
have to do a bunch of things

00:05:30.697 --> 00:05:31.583
before.

00:05:31.583 --> 00:05:33.250
They have to do a
bunch of things later.

00:05:33.250 --> 00:05:34.667
For example, before
patching, they

00:05:34.667 --> 00:05:36.760
have to make sure
everything's working fine.

00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:38.112
The health checks are there.

00:05:38.112 --> 00:05:39.070
The agents are running.

00:05:39.070 --> 00:05:40.188
Users are hydrated.

00:05:40.188 --> 00:05:41.480
Load balance are taken care of.

00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:43.120
There's enough space.

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:45.490
Right after patching
is done, they

00:05:45.490 --> 00:05:47.890
want to make sure their
applications are running.

00:05:47.890 --> 00:05:49.990
They will do the health
checks, services check.

00:05:49.990 --> 00:05:51.370
And that's way they make
sure that they always

00:05:51.370 --> 00:05:52.810
do it in the
maintenance windows,

00:05:52.810 --> 00:05:54.440
like on a Friday
night, Saturday night,

00:05:54.440 --> 00:05:56.230
and they spend a lot of time
to making sure that everything

00:05:56.230 --> 00:05:57.230
is up and running.

00:05:57.230 --> 00:05:59.620
And there is sequencing.

00:05:59.620 --> 00:06:01.420
They want to go automate
these processes.

00:06:01.420 --> 00:06:03.290
When something goes
fine, automatically

00:06:03.290 --> 00:06:04.540
roll out the next environment.

00:06:04.540 --> 00:06:06.610
When the dev environment
goes fine, go to stage.

00:06:06.610 --> 00:06:07.870
So automatically rollout.

00:06:07.870 --> 00:06:11.290
Something goes wrong, stop.

00:06:11.290 --> 00:06:13.930
The other big thing is
application-aware patching.

00:06:13.930 --> 00:06:16.480
So as I said, application is
a key thing for any company.

00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:18.188
And when they're
patching an application,

00:06:18.188 --> 00:06:20.830
they really want to understand
the application architecture.

00:06:20.830 --> 00:06:23.550
So think if you're patching
an end tier application.

00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:25.720
You always want to go
do a back-end first

00:06:25.720 --> 00:06:27.415
or your databases
first then back-end.

00:06:27.415 --> 00:06:29.040
So you do a right
order and everything.

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:31.950
So understanding the
application infrastructure,

00:06:31.950 --> 00:06:35.040
having a knowledge of
that is super important.

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:36.760
The last thing is,
as I talked earlier,

00:06:36.760 --> 00:06:38.128
patching's not something new.

00:06:38.128 --> 00:06:39.920
Our customers have been
patching for years.

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:41.630
So there are a lot
of existing tools.

00:06:41.630 --> 00:06:43.918
So anything we build has to
make sure that it at least

00:06:43.918 --> 00:06:46.210
integrates or provides some
value on top of an existing

00:06:46.210 --> 00:06:49.740
patch management tools.

00:06:49.740 --> 00:06:53.790
So after listening to all these
problems and reprioritizing

00:06:53.790 --> 00:06:55.260
we came up with
a solution, where

00:06:55.260 --> 00:06:57.990
our vision is we want
to go help our customers

00:06:57.990 --> 00:07:00.730
patch any OS anywhere.

00:07:00.730 --> 00:07:02.280
Any OS could be
Windows or Linux.

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:05.390
They could be running
in GCP on GCP.

00:07:05.390 --> 00:07:07.470
But our priority is
right now running in GCP.

00:07:07.470 --> 00:07:09.892
We launched GA in
April this year.

00:07:09.892 --> 00:07:11.225
So that's the General Available.

00:07:11.225 --> 00:07:12.960
You all can go use it.

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:14.280
However, the scope is at GCP.

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:16.760
Both will do Windows and Linux.

00:07:16.760 --> 00:07:17.760
What's a big value prop?

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:21.140
As a customer, why
should I use this?

00:07:21.140 --> 00:07:23.840
Just two main value props
you think about this.

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:26.900
One is, like, I get a
compliance reporting across

00:07:26.900 --> 00:07:30.400
my environments-- across
Windows and Linux and GCP--

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:33.070
telling you what's bad,
how bad it is that.

00:07:33.070 --> 00:07:35.770
Second thing is automated
patch deployment.

00:07:35.770 --> 00:07:37.990
Ability to go fix that
problematic [INAUDIBLE]

00:07:37.990 --> 00:07:40.030
and bring it back
to the compliance.

00:07:40.030 --> 00:07:42.930
And I'm going to go detailed
into each of this stuff there.

00:07:42.930 --> 00:07:47.960
So let's start double-clicking
on what is patch compliance.

00:07:47.960 --> 00:07:51.040
So as I said, when patch Tuesday
comes or any new packages get

00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:52.670
released from the
repo-managers, one

00:07:52.670 --> 00:07:56.360
of the key thing for patch admin
is give me a compliance report

00:07:56.360 --> 00:07:57.470
across Windows and Linux.

00:07:57.470 --> 00:07:59.275
So with our thing,
you got a dashboard.

00:07:59.275 --> 00:08:00.650
As you can see on
the screen, you

00:08:00.650 --> 00:08:03.260
can get a view of which
part of my environment

00:08:03.260 --> 00:08:05.480
is green, which part of
my environment is red,

00:08:05.480 --> 00:08:08.910
which part of it is bad,
along with additional data.

00:08:08.910 --> 00:08:10.550
'Cause when I just
show you a patch,

00:08:10.550 --> 00:08:11.630
compliance is not enough.

00:08:11.630 --> 00:08:14.300
I want to know what's
causing me to read that red.

00:08:14.300 --> 00:08:16.860
What type of patches I am
missing, how many patches

00:08:16.860 --> 00:08:19.490
I'm missing, how long
I've been missing

00:08:19.490 --> 00:08:22.580
because there's an action
item for each of this.

00:08:22.580 --> 00:08:24.900
if I'm running a company.
and I see everything red,

00:08:24.900 --> 00:08:26.270
that's a show stopper to me.

00:08:26.270 --> 00:08:27.830
I'm going to stop
and go fix that.

00:08:27.830 --> 00:08:30.710
If everything is-- most of them
are green, but somewhat yellow,

00:08:30.710 --> 00:08:33.085
probably time for me to take
a weekend off, and come back

00:08:33.085 --> 00:08:36.032
and fix it on the
next business day.

00:08:36.032 --> 00:08:37.490
So that's one big
aspect of getting

00:08:37.490 --> 00:08:39.169
the compliance of
your environment

00:08:39.169 --> 00:08:40.591
with the detailed metadata.

00:08:40.591 --> 00:08:42.049
Second big thing
I want to call out

00:08:42.049 --> 00:08:44.400
is we tried our best
to leverage native

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:46.400
tools, native configuration
rather than agreeing

00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:48.500
to using a lot of neutrals.

00:08:48.500 --> 00:08:51.380
Yes, we do have our own agent
which actually does the job.

00:08:51.380 --> 00:08:53.750
But we actually go talk
to the native Windows

00:08:53.750 --> 00:08:55.970
agent, called Windows
Update Agent or MU

00:08:55.970 --> 00:08:56.890
when we are patching.

00:08:56.890 --> 00:08:59.270
For Linux, we talk to
the native repositories

00:08:59.270 --> 00:09:02.215
like YUM, apt-get, the
repo-managers, and everything.

00:09:02.215 --> 00:09:04.340
And we leverage to whatever
configuration you have,

00:09:04.340 --> 00:09:05.640
whether you're talking
to Microsoft update,

00:09:05.640 --> 00:09:06.530
whether you're talking
to [INAUDIBLE],,

00:09:06.530 --> 00:09:08.330
or whether you're
talking to repositories,

00:09:08.330 --> 00:09:09.770
the key thing is
we're leveraging

00:09:09.770 --> 00:09:13.970
most of the native tools
and config where we can.

00:09:13.970 --> 00:09:16.210
The last thing is on the
supported operating systems.

00:09:16.210 --> 00:09:18.850
For Windows, we
support 2012 and above.

00:09:18.850 --> 00:09:22.160
And Linux we do Red Hat and
[INAUDIBLE] 6, 7, 8 versions,

00:09:22.160 --> 00:09:24.390
Ubuntu 16, 18, and Debian.

00:09:24.390 --> 00:09:26.500
In our documentation,
there's a clear supported

00:09:26.500 --> 00:09:27.838
operating systems link.

00:09:27.838 --> 00:09:29.380
That will be constantly
upgraded when

00:09:29.380 --> 00:09:33.510
we add new operating systems.

00:09:33.510 --> 00:09:34.610
So that's the first part.

00:09:34.610 --> 00:09:37.340
As an admin, I get the
state of my environment,

00:09:37.340 --> 00:09:39.278
getting which one is
good, which one is bad.

00:09:39.278 --> 00:09:40.820
But the next important
thing for them

00:09:40.820 --> 00:09:43.490
is how do I bring in
back to the green state.

00:09:43.490 --> 00:09:44.520
That's my desired state.

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:45.860
How do I bring it back,
everything to green?

00:09:45.860 --> 00:09:48.230
How do I automate this
process so that I don't

00:09:48.230 --> 00:09:51.050
have to worry about everything?

00:09:51.050 --> 00:09:53.610
And that's where the patch
deployment comes after you're

00:09:53.610 --> 00:09:55.510
knowing the compliant state.

00:09:55.510 --> 00:09:57.980
When you're patching
machines or your environment,

00:09:57.980 --> 00:10:01.850
the four important questions
come to any patch admin.

00:10:01.850 --> 00:10:04.220
First thing is-- what
VMs should I patch?

00:10:04.220 --> 00:10:05.990
Should I patch at my
application level?

00:10:05.990 --> 00:10:09.260
Should I patch at my project
level, GCP project or a zone

00:10:09.260 --> 00:10:11.420
level, or a particular
region level?

00:10:11.420 --> 00:10:15.017
So we provide you all the
flexible targeting options.

00:10:15.017 --> 00:10:17.350
You could choose to patch all
web servers because that's

00:10:17.350 --> 00:10:18.290
how you labeled it.

00:10:18.290 --> 00:10:20.570
Or you could patch all
my dev environment.

00:10:20.570 --> 00:10:22.070
So you have all the
flexible filters

00:10:22.070 --> 00:10:23.660
you can choose the
way you want, which

00:10:23.660 --> 00:10:27.380
I'll be demoing in a few
minutes when I go there.

00:10:27.380 --> 00:10:29.690
Second big important
question an admin

00:10:29.690 --> 00:10:32.450
will as is what type
of patch is installed?

00:10:32.450 --> 00:10:33.738
So first one is what VMs.

00:10:33.738 --> 00:10:34.280
That's great.

00:10:34.280 --> 00:10:36.470
Second-- what type of
patches I want to install.

00:10:36.470 --> 00:10:39.290
Should I install critical,
security, are others?

00:10:39.290 --> 00:10:41.990
Because, as I said, every
company have different SLS.

00:10:41.990 --> 00:10:43.340
That's what we've heard.

00:10:43.340 --> 00:10:45.270
Windows is 30 days
saved, for example.

00:10:45.270 --> 00:10:46.740
The next could be
60 days, 90 days.

00:10:46.740 --> 00:10:47.870
So every company differs.

00:10:47.870 --> 00:10:51.620
But on average, every company
have different SLS to go patch.

00:10:51.620 --> 00:10:54.680
So it depends on the type of
patches you want to go select.

00:10:54.680 --> 00:10:57.140
You need to have an option
to select type of patches.

00:10:57.140 --> 00:11:00.240
That's the patch configuration
I'm talking about.

00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:02.170
A couple of key
things to hit here--

00:11:02.170 --> 00:11:03.700
patch exclusion.

00:11:03.700 --> 00:11:06.803
And many times when you
have a particular patch,

00:11:06.803 --> 00:11:09.220
it might break some of your
line-of-business applications,

00:11:09.220 --> 00:11:11.780
or there could be some
incompatibility issues.

00:11:11.780 --> 00:11:13.940
So you might want to
exclude certain patches

00:11:13.940 --> 00:11:16.180
because you know that's
going to break something.

00:11:16.180 --> 00:11:17.950
So there's a patch
exclusion capability

00:11:17.950 --> 00:11:21.367
where you can specify a KB
article or a wildcard in Linux

00:11:21.367 --> 00:11:23.200
like [INAUDIBLE] kernel
if you really wanted

00:11:23.200 --> 00:11:25.210
to install kernel patches.

00:11:25.210 --> 00:11:26.920
There's a patch exclusion.

00:11:26.920 --> 00:11:30.070
Similarly, there
is patch inclusion.

00:11:30.070 --> 00:11:33.190
Think of a scenario where
you've got a security patch,

00:11:33.190 --> 00:11:36.780
and you want that to roll out
to your environment immediately.

00:11:36.780 --> 00:11:38.280
And you just put that patch.

00:11:38.280 --> 00:11:40.780
Include only this patch and
roll out all my environment.

00:11:40.780 --> 00:11:43.730
So that's the patch
inclusion capabilities.

00:11:43.730 --> 00:11:45.940
So the first question
was, what VMs to patch.

00:11:45.940 --> 00:11:48.580
Second thing was what
patches to apply.

00:11:48.580 --> 00:11:50.290
Third big question
comes to customers as,

00:11:50.290 --> 00:11:51.665
like, when should I patch?

00:11:51.665 --> 00:11:53.040
Patching is not
a one-time thing.

00:11:53.040 --> 00:11:54.040
I'll be doing regularly.

00:11:54.040 --> 00:11:55.570
I could be doing,
like, first day

00:11:55.570 --> 00:11:57.070
of the month, last
day of the month,

00:11:57.070 --> 00:11:58.320
the last Friday of the months.

00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:00.310
So we provide all
those flexible options.

00:12:00.310 --> 00:12:01.652
You can do now.

00:12:01.652 --> 00:12:02.360
You can one time.

00:12:02.360 --> 00:12:03.610
You can do regularly.

00:12:03.610 --> 00:12:05.650
And you can choose different
dates, times, weeks

00:12:05.650 --> 00:12:06.817
of the days, and everything.

00:12:06.817 --> 00:12:10.070
I'll go a lot more
details when I demo that.

00:12:10.070 --> 00:12:12.380
The last part of scheduling
is the maintenance window

00:12:12.380 --> 00:12:13.520
enforcement.

00:12:13.520 --> 00:12:15.740
Almost 90% of our
customers told me

00:12:15.740 --> 00:12:18.050
that they'll patch only
during a maintenance window

00:12:18.050 --> 00:12:20.780
as we see people do it typically
on the Friday nights, Saturday

00:12:20.780 --> 00:12:22.737
nights, or on the weekends.

00:12:22.737 --> 00:12:24.320
So they have a
maintenance window set,

00:12:24.320 --> 00:12:26.660
and they want to doing
the patch deployment only

00:12:26.660 --> 00:12:27.750
during that time.

00:12:27.750 --> 00:12:30.680
So in our product, you can
specify a window saying that,

00:12:30.680 --> 00:12:32.810
hey, start at 9:00 in
the night on a Friday,

00:12:32.810 --> 00:12:34.710
but run for only
for three hours.

00:12:34.710 --> 00:12:37.430
So we'll ensure that we run
only that three hours, including

00:12:37.430 --> 00:12:38.047
the reboot.

00:12:38.047 --> 00:12:39.630
And we finish out
what we are running.

00:12:39.630 --> 00:12:41.005
And we get out
off to process, so

00:12:41.005 --> 00:12:44.710
that you're app's up and
up and running and coming.

00:12:44.710 --> 00:12:47.262
After the key questions--
what VMs, what patches,

00:12:47.262 --> 00:12:49.720
went should I patch-- the last
important thing is controls,

00:12:49.720 --> 00:12:51.670
what additional
controls I would need.

00:12:51.670 --> 00:12:53.710
I talked about a lot
of batch orchestration.

00:12:53.710 --> 00:12:55.560
That's one of the
top concerns what we

00:12:55.560 --> 00:12:57.660
have heard from our customers.

00:12:57.660 --> 00:13:00.700
One of the big thing is
ability to run pre-steps--

00:13:00.700 --> 00:13:01.870
something before patch.

00:13:01.870 --> 00:13:04.090
It could be health
checks or disk space.

00:13:04.090 --> 00:13:05.840
And something to
run after the patch.

00:13:05.840 --> 00:13:09.220
So in our UI, you will be able
to select certain prescripts.

00:13:09.220 --> 00:13:12.310
This could be a script which
you upload to a Google cloud

00:13:12.310 --> 00:13:13.600
storage GCS pocket.

00:13:13.600 --> 00:13:15.550
And it'll pull up from
there and will run it.

00:13:15.550 --> 00:13:17.200
And it can also condition it.

00:13:17.200 --> 00:13:19.685
If the script fails, the
patch in deployment will stop.

00:13:19.685 --> 00:13:22.060
So you do not need having to
worry about, like, hey, have

00:13:22.060 --> 00:13:24.865
a messed up something there?

00:13:24.865 --> 00:13:27.490
The last part of the control was
one of the major feedback what

00:13:27.490 --> 00:13:30.460
we have heard from beta when
we released early this year was

00:13:30.460 --> 00:13:33.590
reboot control, especially when
you're patching applications.

00:13:33.590 --> 00:13:36.083
Even though we do a controlled
reboot, as a patch admin,

00:13:36.083 --> 00:13:37.750
I want to control my
reboot because it's

00:13:37.750 --> 00:13:39.190
my application running.

00:13:39.190 --> 00:13:42.070
So we don't reboot if the
user chooses this selection.

00:13:42.070 --> 00:13:43.420
It's a configuration option.

00:13:43.420 --> 00:13:44.230
We will not reboot.

00:13:44.230 --> 00:13:45.980
We will hand over the
control to the user.

00:13:45.980 --> 00:13:47.670
So after the patching
is done, they

00:13:47.670 --> 00:13:52.415
can come and control the
reboot per their needs.

00:13:52.415 --> 00:13:54.540
So that's about the two
value props I talked about.

00:13:54.540 --> 00:13:56.590
One is this
patch-compliant state,

00:13:56.590 --> 00:13:58.150
giving you the state
of environment,

00:13:58.150 --> 00:13:59.722
how-it-is-right-now snapshot.

00:13:59.722 --> 00:14:01.180
And the second
thing is the ability

00:14:01.180 --> 00:14:02.948
to go fix that and
automate this, so

00:14:02.948 --> 00:14:04.240
future you don't have to do it.

00:14:04.240 --> 00:14:07.770
Those are the two big value
props of patch management.

00:14:07.770 --> 00:14:08.910
Enough of me talking.

00:14:08.910 --> 00:14:12.240
I really want to show you how
the product looks in the UI.

00:14:12.240 --> 00:14:14.460
So in this
patch-management demo,

00:14:14.460 --> 00:14:16.750
I'm going to walk you
through three things.

00:14:16.750 --> 00:14:18.580
The first thing is
how do you onboard,

00:14:18.580 --> 00:14:21.300
navigate an onboard
patch management service.

00:14:21.300 --> 00:14:24.340
Second thing is, how do you
get the patch compliance.

00:14:24.340 --> 00:14:26.930
And third thing is how do
you or do a patch deployment,

00:14:26.930 --> 00:14:29.130
basically go fixing those
problematic machines

00:14:29.130 --> 00:14:31.380
and bringing it back
to this compliance.

00:14:31.380 --> 00:14:33.140
And you can automate
this process.

00:14:33.140 --> 00:14:35.020
OK, how do I navigate?

00:14:35.020 --> 00:14:39.960
So if I go to my Google Console,
go here, go to Compute Engine.

00:14:39.960 --> 00:14:43.300
And on the bottom, you can
see OS Patch Management.

00:14:43.300 --> 00:14:44.850
And this is the
screen what you will

00:14:44.850 --> 00:14:47.600
see, 'cause you have not
enabled the services.

00:14:47.600 --> 00:14:49.980
And you can see what APIs
we need, what permissions.

00:14:49.980 --> 00:14:51.240
we need.

00:14:51.240 --> 00:14:52.950
And if you click
on Learn More, it

00:14:52.950 --> 00:14:54.593
talks about whole
details about what

00:14:54.593 --> 00:14:56.760
is the service, how you can
enable it, how it works,

00:14:56.760 --> 00:14:58.920
what's architecture,
what does its agent do,

00:14:58.920 --> 00:15:02.430
what permissions--
all the details.

00:15:02.430 --> 00:15:05.260
Once you enable the service
and activate the agent,

00:15:05.260 --> 00:15:09.570
the OS config agent is part of
the most of the GC base image

00:15:09.570 --> 00:15:11.760
packages, except, I
think, Ubuntu and Suzy,

00:15:11.760 --> 00:15:13.620
which we are working
to add right now.

00:15:13.620 --> 00:15:16.230
But those things, you
have to do it separately.

00:15:16.230 --> 00:15:20.640
And we have a clear instructions
how to go install an agent,

00:15:20.640 --> 00:15:23.220
especially after if you
are using an older VMs,

00:15:23.220 --> 00:15:25.350
you have to follow the
instructions of how

00:15:25.350 --> 00:15:28.000
to go get this OS
configuration onto the VMs.

00:15:28.000 --> 00:15:29.308
So now we enable the services.

00:15:29.308 --> 00:15:31.350
In the interest of time,
I've already enabled it.

00:15:31.350 --> 00:15:35.980
I'll go to a project where I've
already enabled the service.

00:15:35.980 --> 00:15:40.050
So once you enable and activate,
we'll go scan all your VMs,

00:15:40.050 --> 00:15:42.130
collect the data
from all the sources,

00:15:42.130 --> 00:15:46.148
aggregate it, and
generate these reports.

00:15:46.148 --> 00:15:47.940
Let me walk you through
each of this stuff.

00:15:47.940 --> 00:15:51.060
The first tile talks
about all the VMs

00:15:51.060 --> 00:15:52.620
I have in my operating systems.

00:15:52.620 --> 00:15:55.200
Looks like I've got a thing 18
in this environment who have

00:15:55.200 --> 00:15:57.570
different operating systems.

00:15:57.570 --> 00:15:59.220
Looks like my Ubuntu is good.

00:15:59.220 --> 00:16:00.490
It's not missing any red.

00:16:00.490 --> 00:16:01.800
So I don't have a showstopper.

00:16:01.800 --> 00:16:02.730
I can move on.

00:16:02.730 --> 00:16:03.940
Looks like CentOS is bad.

00:16:03.940 --> 00:16:06.023
I've got all the different
categories I'm missing.

00:16:06.023 --> 00:16:07.710
I'm missing critical,
important, other,

00:16:07.710 --> 00:16:09.240
and a few are up to date.

00:16:09.240 --> 00:16:10.800
Similarly with Windows.

00:16:10.800 --> 00:16:12.270
And Red Hat is also bad.

00:16:12.270 --> 00:16:13.765
And if you want
to know more what

00:16:13.765 --> 00:16:16.020
are this type categories,
what does each one mean,

00:16:16.020 --> 00:16:17.610
if you click on this
particular link,

00:16:17.610 --> 00:16:20.190
it'll take your documentation.

00:16:20.190 --> 00:16:22.060
Let me just navigate there.

00:16:22.060 --> 00:16:23.790
So if you go to our
documentation page,

00:16:23.790 --> 00:16:26.820
it talks about the whole
charts what you see there

00:16:26.820 --> 00:16:29.430
and explaining what
each one means and how

00:16:29.430 --> 00:16:30.730
do we compute that.

00:16:30.730 --> 00:16:31.230
.

00:16:31.230 --> 00:16:33.520
All right, going back
to the dashboard.

00:16:33.520 --> 00:16:35.680
If I'm an admin and
looking at this,

00:16:35.680 --> 00:16:37.320
I really want to
know why is this bad.

00:16:37.320 --> 00:16:40.830
Let me know which VMs
are having problems

00:16:40.830 --> 00:16:44.490
and what sort of things
we already have this here.

00:16:44.490 --> 00:16:49.167
OK, so you can also click on the
VM instance page as well here.

00:16:49.167 --> 00:16:50.250
So you see the same thing.

00:16:50.250 --> 00:16:54.090
And here's where you'll see the
different VMs, what you have.

00:16:54.090 --> 00:16:56.500
Part of that is--

00:16:56.500 --> 00:16:58.668
let me pick up the
[INAUDIBLE] CentOS

00:16:58.668 --> 00:17:00.960
VM it's having, and you can
see all the metadata here--

00:17:00.960 --> 00:17:03.640
like what zone it is, what
OS distribution it's running,

00:17:03.640 --> 00:17:04.833
what OS version.

00:17:04.833 --> 00:17:06.750
And saying that it's
missing critical updates.

00:17:06.750 --> 00:17:08.609
When you click on
that, that's where

00:17:08.609 --> 00:17:11.329
it shows that, oh, I'm missing
