00:17:11.329 --> 00:17:12.780
Three of them are critical.

00:17:12.780 --> 00:17:15.630
The other things are
security and other important,

00:17:15.630 --> 00:17:17.349
something for me
to take an action.

00:17:17.349 --> 00:17:20.742
So in upcoming releases, we
are adding a lot of metadata.

00:17:20.742 --> 00:17:22.200
I have to show you
a lot more data,

00:17:22.200 --> 00:17:24.597
like CB score, what repo
manager I can connect to,

00:17:24.597 --> 00:17:26.930
where do I get this patch--
it's a lot more information.

00:17:26.930 --> 00:17:29.310
In Windows we are adding
that key billings, the MSR

00:17:29.310 --> 00:17:29.810
facilities.

00:17:29.810 --> 00:17:33.540
We'll get a lot more metadata
in our upcoming releases.

00:17:33.540 --> 00:17:36.850
So that completes
the first part,

00:17:36.850 --> 00:17:38.850
where we have seen--
actually, two parts.

00:17:38.850 --> 00:17:40.780
First we have shown
about onboarding.

00:17:40.780 --> 00:17:42.270
We have seen how
to get the patch

00:17:42.270 --> 00:17:45.190
compliance in an environment
state of your environment.

00:17:45.190 --> 00:17:46.860
The third thing
is as an admin, I

00:17:46.860 --> 00:17:48.720
want to go fix this
problematic machines.

00:17:48.720 --> 00:17:51.600
I want to go create a deployment
so that it installs the patches

00:17:51.600 --> 00:17:54.700
and brings these VMs back
to the compliant state.

00:17:54.700 --> 00:17:59.050
So now I'm going to click
on Patch Deployment.

00:17:59.050 --> 00:18:00.638
So as I talked
earlier in the slide,

00:18:00.638 --> 00:18:03.180
there are four questions which
really matter for patch admins

00:18:03.180 --> 00:18:04.472
when you're doing a deployment.

00:18:04.472 --> 00:18:06.660
First is what VMs to patch.

00:18:06.660 --> 00:18:09.570
And we give a lot of flexibility
to choose different filtering

00:18:09.570 --> 00:18:13.630
mechanisms, whether we can
patch by a project, a zone--

00:18:13.630 --> 00:18:16.390
I'm talking about a GCP
project zone, region.

00:18:16.390 --> 00:18:19.440
Or you an use labels,
tags, name prefixes.

00:18:19.440 --> 00:18:21.360
In this case, I'm using
same labels called

00:18:21.360 --> 00:18:23.880
A-- environment have all my
definitions I want to go patch.

00:18:23.880 --> 00:18:27.250
Or I can choose
to patch all VMs.

00:18:27.250 --> 00:18:31.950
OK, we've finished the first
question of what VMs to patch.

00:18:31.950 --> 00:18:33.720
Second important
question is what

00:18:33.720 --> 00:18:37.830
patches I want to install
because every customer will

00:18:37.830 --> 00:18:41.580
have different SLS
for different patches.

00:18:41.580 --> 00:18:43.800
I've seen most of the
customers have critical

00:18:43.800 --> 00:18:45.970
and security have to
be done within 30 days.

00:18:45.970 --> 00:18:48.360
If it is non-critical, they
have a little bit more time--

00:18:48.360 --> 00:18:49.770
45 days. there.

00:18:49.770 --> 00:18:51.270
So we give you all
the flexibility,

00:18:51.270 --> 00:18:54.150
so you can choose what
sort of patches you want.

00:18:54.150 --> 00:18:55.750
Like, for example,
critical security,

00:18:55.750 --> 00:18:58.010
that's the only
thing I care about.

00:18:58.010 --> 00:19:01.570
Patch exclusion-- so this one
I was talking about earlier.

00:19:01.570 --> 00:19:04.202
If you know that a
particular patch--

00:19:04.202 --> 00:19:05.910
or KB in this thing--
it's going to break

00:19:05.910 --> 00:19:07.420
your line-of-business
application

00:19:07.420 --> 00:19:09.590
or some incompatibility
with a known thing,

00:19:09.590 --> 00:19:12.620
you can put that KB article, you
can put multiple KB articles,

00:19:12.620 --> 00:19:13.950
comma separated.

00:19:13.950 --> 00:19:15.890
We will exclude that
when you are installing

00:19:15.890 --> 00:19:17.690
the remaining patches there.

00:19:17.690 --> 00:19:20.000
In Linux, we can do,
like, kernel updates,

00:19:20.000 --> 00:19:21.140
or you can do wildcards.

00:19:21.140 --> 00:19:24.290
You can use any of
those stuff there.

00:19:24.290 --> 00:19:26.610
But other than critical
patch inclusion,

00:19:26.610 --> 00:19:29.850
you just want to install
selective updates.

00:19:29.850 --> 00:19:31.675
Microsoft released
a security patch,

00:19:31.675 --> 00:19:33.800
or they've got a separate
package from the next one

00:19:33.800 --> 00:19:36.050
that I want to install
that particular security

00:19:36.050 --> 00:19:40.010
package on my fleet right
now or as soon as possible.

00:19:40.010 --> 00:19:42.650
So you can include that
particular KB article,

00:19:42.650 --> 00:19:44.638
or the Linux, or
whatever the wildcard

00:19:44.638 --> 00:19:45.680
you are using to do that.

00:19:45.680 --> 00:19:49.370
Similarly, we do that same
thing for both Windows and Linux

00:19:49.370 --> 00:19:52.300
whether it's running YUM,
Red Hat, CentOS, or APT,

00:19:52.300 --> 00:19:54.410
you can choose your own
configuration and do that.

00:19:54.410 --> 00:19:56.060
So that finishes
the second question

00:19:56.060 --> 00:20:00.080
what we had-- number of VMs,
what type of VMs, what patches.

00:20:00.080 --> 00:20:02.930
Third question comes to patch
admins as when should I patch?

00:20:02.930 --> 00:20:04.462
Patching is not
something I do once.

00:20:04.462 --> 00:20:05.420
It's a recurring thing.

00:20:05.420 --> 00:20:09.260
So I need to give me all the
flexibilities to do it now,

00:20:09.260 --> 00:20:10.680
do it later.

00:20:10.680 --> 00:20:12.800
So for example, if I
choose End of the month

00:20:12.800 --> 00:20:16.400
to [INAUDIBLE] or I want to
be in a particular recurring

00:20:16.400 --> 00:20:19.730
schedule, like, I want to
do it on a specific day.

00:20:19.730 --> 00:20:23.302
I want to do it on a
fourth Friday of the month,

00:20:23.302 --> 00:20:25.010
or I can do second
Saturday of the month,

00:20:25.010 --> 00:20:27.390
second Tuesday of the month,
aligning patch Tuesday.

00:20:27.390 --> 00:20:30.050
So whatever means
you have, we provided

00:20:30.050 --> 00:20:32.207
flexible recurring
scheduling options.

00:20:32.207 --> 00:20:34.040
So you can choose
whatever is right for you.

00:20:34.040 --> 00:20:36.350
In my case, I'm going to
choose scheduled for later

00:20:36.350 --> 00:20:38.500
and doing end of the month.

00:20:38.500 --> 00:20:41.540
The other key thing to
talk about is duration.

00:20:41.540 --> 00:20:43.885
Almost every customer
confirmed that they're

00:20:43.885 --> 00:20:45.260
going to install
the patches only

00:20:45.260 --> 00:20:46.880
during the maintenance window.

00:20:46.880 --> 00:20:50.010
Typically happens on a Friday
night or on a weekend there.

00:20:50.010 --> 00:20:52.700
So they want to make sure that
the app is down at that time.

00:20:52.700 --> 00:20:54.360
No user is connected to that.

00:20:54.360 --> 00:20:56.870
So here, you can specify
a maintenance window,

00:20:56.870 --> 00:20:58.190
like a duration--

00:20:58.190 --> 00:21:00.062
how long you want to
install the patches.

00:21:00.062 --> 00:21:01.520
If you on install
the patches, say,

00:21:01.520 --> 00:21:03.530
for 120 minutes,
that's only time

00:21:03.530 --> 00:21:06.880
we'll be installing the
patches, including the reboot.

00:21:06.880 --> 00:21:07.880
We'll take care of that.

00:21:07.880 --> 00:21:10.450
However, we'll finish
whatever the step is there.

00:21:10.450 --> 00:21:12.060
We'll not start
any new step there.

00:21:15.780 --> 00:21:18.390
The last thing which every
patch admin will care about

00:21:18.390 --> 00:21:20.430
is rebooting.

00:21:20.430 --> 00:21:22.560
By default, the
system will decide

00:21:22.560 --> 00:21:24.810
if the reboot is necessary
based on the metadata, what

00:21:24.810 --> 00:21:27.360
I'm getting from the
package or packages.

00:21:27.360 --> 00:21:31.530
But some admins, like, I want
to control my reboot, especially

00:21:31.530 --> 00:21:32.820
in patching.

00:21:32.820 --> 00:21:34.800
My application, I
want to make sure

00:21:34.800 --> 00:21:36.870
that this is actually working.

00:21:36.870 --> 00:21:38.180
I want to be in front of it.

00:21:38.180 --> 00:21:40.097
And I want to run some
presteps and poststeps.

00:21:40.097 --> 00:21:42.075
So we gave you that
options to choose never,

00:21:42.075 --> 00:21:43.200
so that you can control it.

00:21:43.200 --> 00:21:45.780
For now, I'll leave it
as a default system.

00:21:45.780 --> 00:21:47.740
Next, comes the pre
and post scripts.

00:21:47.740 --> 00:21:48.630
This is what I
was talking about.

00:21:48.630 --> 00:21:50.255
A lot of admins want
to run some things

00:21:50.255 --> 00:21:52.287
before to make sure
everything's working fine.

00:21:52.287 --> 00:21:54.870
They want to run a lot of things
after to make sure everything

00:21:54.870 --> 00:21:57.408
is working fine there.

00:21:57.408 --> 00:21:58.200
How do you do that?

00:21:58.200 --> 00:22:02.280
So you create your script,
upload your GCS packet--

00:22:02.280 --> 00:22:03.630
Google Cloud Storage packet.

00:22:03.630 --> 00:22:05.160
In our documentation,
we explain how

00:22:05.160 --> 00:22:07.380
do you get that script
into the packet.

00:22:07.380 --> 00:22:09.930
Now I can go click on that.

00:22:09.930 --> 00:22:11.460
It opens up a GCS packet.

00:22:11.460 --> 00:22:12.710
And I can go select.

00:22:12.710 --> 00:22:16.920
Hey, I want to go do
this, my Linux prescript.

00:22:16.920 --> 00:22:19.630
And normally, you want
to do some postscript.

00:22:19.630 --> 00:22:24.750
You want to go select this,
and you want to go build this.

00:22:24.750 --> 00:22:26.582
Then you create your deployment.

00:22:26.582 --> 00:22:29.040
So let me show you what happens
if you create a deployment.

00:22:29.040 --> 00:22:31.540
For the interest of time, I've
already created a few things.

00:22:31.540 --> 00:22:33.630
That's where we go to
Schedule Deployments.

00:22:33.630 --> 00:22:37.110
So once you create a deployment,
you can actually see it here.

00:22:37.110 --> 00:22:39.187
Right now, we have an
option only to delete,

00:22:39.187 --> 00:22:41.020
recreate it if you want
to make any changes.

00:22:41.020 --> 00:22:43.410
But in the near future,
we'll be adding capabilities

00:22:43.410 --> 00:22:44.830
where you can edit.

00:22:44.830 --> 00:22:46.080
You want to add more machines.

00:22:46.080 --> 00:22:47.820
You want add in
the configuration.

00:22:47.820 --> 00:22:48.987
You want to change the date.

00:22:48.987 --> 00:22:52.230
You will be able to
do all the changes.

00:22:52.230 --> 00:22:53.190
Again, I created it.

00:22:53.190 --> 00:22:55.740
When the time triggers,
the patch deployment runs,

00:22:55.740 --> 00:22:58.150
and it'll give you a
detailed patch status.

00:22:58.150 --> 00:23:00.340
So let me show you
an example there.

00:23:00.340 --> 00:23:03.700
So I've run some patch jobs
last week, earlier this week.

00:23:03.700 --> 00:23:05.400
And it actually
shows all completed.

00:23:05.400 --> 00:23:06.490
But there are some others.

00:23:06.490 --> 00:23:09.060
So let me pick up a deployment
which had a lot of VMs,

00:23:09.060 --> 00:23:09.960
and it failed.

00:23:09.960 --> 00:23:15.090
So let me take this one which
has got 16 VMs, and it failed.

00:23:15.090 --> 00:23:16.980
First, it talks about
a number of incidents

00:23:16.980 --> 00:23:18.420
it has-- over 11 status.

00:23:18.420 --> 00:23:19.920
And something errors occurred.

00:23:19.920 --> 00:23:22.080
As an admin here, please
take an action here.

00:23:22.080 --> 00:23:23.110
Look into that.

00:23:23.110 --> 00:23:24.930
How long was the
duration wanted to run?

00:23:24.930 --> 00:23:26.700
It will give you
all the details.

00:23:26.700 --> 00:23:28.070
If anything was left in between.

00:23:28.070 --> 00:23:31.470
It was 100% done.

00:23:31.470 --> 00:23:33.223
And what classifications
you have selected

00:23:33.223 --> 00:23:34.140
gives all the details.

00:23:34.140 --> 00:23:36.360
Now this is the result state.

00:23:36.360 --> 00:23:40.140
If you look at in this state,
some are successful in that 16.

00:23:40.140 --> 00:23:42.057
Some failed because
there's no agent detected.

00:23:42.057 --> 00:23:43.973
Somewhere there was
configuration that was not

00:23:43.973 --> 00:23:45.307
running, or something happened.

00:23:45.307 --> 00:23:47.640
Somewhat inactive, which means
that the machine could be

00:23:47.640 --> 00:23:48.932
offline, or something happened.

00:23:48.932 --> 00:23:49.915
It's not running.

00:23:49.915 --> 00:23:51.540
So there could be
other state in there.

00:23:51.540 --> 00:23:54.490
Or the action failed because
of incorrect function

00:23:54.490 --> 00:23:55.340
for the batch.

00:23:55.340 --> 00:23:56.620
But here is a clear logs.

00:23:56.620 --> 00:23:58.490
If you click on any
of the logs, you

00:23:58.490 --> 00:24:01.080
can clearly see all the
details about this failure, why

00:24:01.080 --> 00:24:03.000
it failed, what time
it failed-- typical

00:24:03.000 --> 00:24:04.040
logs you get that stuff.

00:24:04.040 --> 00:24:06.040
And this is where you can
go to our stack driver

00:24:06.040 --> 00:24:09.390
logs to get a lot more details.

00:24:09.390 --> 00:24:11.700
OK, so that completes
the three parts

00:24:11.700 --> 00:24:13.230
I was talking about from patch--

00:24:13.230 --> 00:24:16.020
how do you navigate an
onboard patch management,

00:24:16.020 --> 00:24:18.730
how to get the compliant
state of your environment,

00:24:18.730 --> 00:24:21.960
and finally, how
to deploy patches

00:24:21.960 --> 00:24:24.630
to install those patches so that
you bring that machines back

00:24:24.630 --> 00:24:28.180
into the compliance and set an
automated recurring deployment,

00:24:28.180 --> 00:24:30.740
so every time, it
takes care of that.

00:24:30.740 --> 00:24:33.160
Now let's talk about
the next service--

00:24:33.160 --> 00:24:35.210
so on the configuration
management.

00:24:35.210 --> 00:24:36.960
There's another service
what we prioritize

00:24:36.960 --> 00:24:39.570
based on our customers need
and also keeping compliance

00:24:39.570 --> 00:24:41.500
in mind.

00:24:41.500 --> 00:24:43.640
So let's start with what
are the top problems what

00:24:43.640 --> 00:24:47.360
we have heard with our
customers when we spoke to them.

00:24:47.360 --> 00:24:51.220
The first thing is, when you
go create your VM environment,

00:24:51.220 --> 00:24:53.470
you want that to be in
a certain desired state.

00:24:53.470 --> 00:24:55.810
Like, I want security agents.

00:24:55.810 --> 00:24:58.270
I want monitoring agents
to collect all the logs.

00:24:58.270 --> 00:25:01.480
I want open certain
firewalls, certain posts,

00:25:01.480 --> 00:25:04.380
certain accounts, or you want
to have certain software.

00:25:04.380 --> 00:25:05.740
So that's the first part.

00:25:05.740 --> 00:25:07.910
You define your
desired configuration.

00:25:07.910 --> 00:25:10.360
The second big thing is
how do I do deploys this

00:25:10.360 --> 00:25:12.740
to entire environment at scale?

00:25:12.740 --> 00:25:14.690
How do I deploy this at scale?

00:25:14.690 --> 00:25:16.220
Third big thing
is maintaining it.

00:25:16.220 --> 00:25:17.853
It's not about
deploying it one time.

00:25:17.853 --> 00:25:18.520
That's all good.

00:25:18.520 --> 00:25:21.550
But how do I ensure that
this is maintaining?

00:25:21.550 --> 00:25:23.380
I don't want any drifts.

00:25:23.380 --> 00:25:26.860
Whenever there is a drift, as a
user, it I want to be notified.

00:25:26.860 --> 00:25:28.780
And, obviously, I want
to auto-fix it later

00:25:28.780 --> 00:25:30.100
stage once I get a context.

00:25:30.100 --> 00:25:32.800
But at the minimum, I want to
be notified if there is a drift.

00:25:32.800 --> 00:25:34.420
What drifted Who caused drift?

00:25:34.420 --> 00:25:35.122
What happened?

00:25:35.122 --> 00:25:35.830
Why it was drift.

00:25:35.830 --> 00:25:36.830
How long it's been drifted.

00:25:36.830 --> 00:25:38.650
So you need to know
all those details, what

00:25:38.650 --> 00:25:41.170
we call a complianced
reporting, from a configuration

00:25:41.170 --> 00:25:42.340
standpoint.

00:25:42.340 --> 00:25:44.920
The other big thing they'll
ask in a unified way,

00:25:44.920 --> 00:25:47.080
whether I'm bootstrapping
or installing

00:25:47.080 --> 00:25:49.570
a Google first-party software
or a third-party software,

00:25:49.570 --> 00:25:52.180
I need a unified way.

00:25:52.180 --> 00:25:55.480
The other big thing what we
have heard from our customers

00:25:55.480 --> 00:25:59.890
how do we integrate with
existing CICD tools?

00:25:59.890 --> 00:26:01.390
For example, most
of our customers

00:26:01.390 --> 00:26:03.880
use different
third-party CICD tools,

00:26:03.880 --> 00:26:06.490
like whether it's Jenkins,
Packard, Spinnaker-- any

00:26:06.490 --> 00:26:08.230
that they have their
own tools there.

00:26:08.230 --> 00:26:10.570
They use our tools to
go create the packages.

00:26:10.570 --> 00:26:12.820
And key thing is they
want to take that package

00:26:12.820 --> 00:26:14.950
and deploying to that
environment at scale

00:26:14.950 --> 00:26:16.980
and maintain it.

00:26:16.980 --> 00:26:19.135
So these are some
of the problems.

00:26:19.135 --> 00:26:20.510
To solve some of
the problems, we

00:26:20.510 --> 00:26:23.360
started creating
configuration management,

00:26:23.360 --> 00:26:25.970
where our value prop is
going to be three things--

00:26:25.970 --> 00:26:29.460
define, deploy, and maintain.

00:26:29.460 --> 00:26:30.500
So what does it mean?

00:26:30.500 --> 00:26:33.650
We'll help you to define
your configuration file.

00:26:33.650 --> 00:26:36.360
Second step is we'll help
you to deploy at scale,

00:26:36.360 --> 00:26:37.463
your entire environment.

00:26:37.463 --> 00:26:39.380
The same sort of filters
what I taught earlier

00:26:39.380 --> 00:26:42.860
in patch management, where you
can do it at a project level,

00:26:42.860 --> 00:26:44.780
at a region level,
at a zone level,

00:26:44.780 --> 00:26:46.850
or a different instance level
what I talked about-- basically

00:26:46.850 --> 00:26:48.230
giving a lot of
flexible options,

00:26:48.230 --> 00:26:51.705
so that you can choose how
you want to deploy that.

00:26:51.705 --> 00:26:53.580
Going to give you a nice
compliance dashboard

00:26:53.580 --> 00:26:54.600
across Windows and Linux.

00:26:54.600 --> 00:26:56.790
They'll be similar to what
we have done with the patch.

00:26:56.790 --> 00:26:58.623
And with a lot of
additional data, as a user

00:26:58.623 --> 00:27:02.010
you know which machines are in
the compliance, which machines

00:27:02.010 --> 00:27:03.825
went out of the
compliance, what caused

00:27:03.825 --> 00:27:06.570
it to go out of the compliance,
when it actually drifted.

00:27:06.570 --> 00:27:08.310
So you get a lot
more details on that.

00:27:08.310 --> 00:27:11.678
So let me walk you through
how the product works.

00:27:11.678 --> 00:27:13.220
From a user standpoint,
when you have

00:27:13.220 --> 00:27:15.090
to go start
configuration management,

00:27:15.090 --> 00:27:16.632
there are two things
you think about.

00:27:16.632 --> 00:27:18.440
One is I'm creating
my desired config.

00:27:18.440 --> 00:27:20.690
There's a thing called that
as simple YAML file, where

00:27:20.690 --> 00:27:23.330
you define your
desired config installs

00:27:23.330 --> 00:27:26.003
a particular monitoring agent,
installs security agent,

00:27:26.003 --> 00:27:26.795
open this firewall.

00:27:26.795 --> 00:27:28.880
So you define all that stuff.

00:27:28.880 --> 00:27:31.760
We call that as a guest
policy in our terms

00:27:31.760 --> 00:27:35.666
because you're applying
a policy to the guest VM.

00:27:35.666 --> 00:27:39.010
Second big step
is take the policy

00:27:39.010 --> 00:27:42.560
and apply what we call
assignment to the bunch of VMs.

00:27:42.560 --> 00:27:45.610
So let me double-click
on each of the stuff.

00:27:45.610 --> 00:27:49.537
Guest policy-- when I'm defining
my policy or the configuration,

00:27:49.537 --> 00:27:51.870
you might want to say what
packages you want to install.

00:27:51.870 --> 00:27:53.710
All this is part of
the guest policy.

00:27:53.710 --> 00:27:55.900
For example, in this example,
took an example of it.

00:27:55.900 --> 00:27:56.970
I want to install
a Stack driver,

00:27:56.970 --> 00:27:58.700
and I want that to
be always installed.

00:27:58.700 --> 00:28:00.640
My desired state is
always to be installed,

00:28:00.640 --> 00:28:02.210
as you can see in the screen.

00:28:02.210 --> 00:28:04.230
So in the same guest
policy, now you

00:28:04.230 --> 00:28:07.388
define where do I get this
package binaries from.

00:28:07.388 --> 00:28:08.680
I want to install Stack driver.

00:28:08.680 --> 00:28:09.222
That's great.

00:28:09.222 --> 00:28:11.910
But go pull it out
from this, the order

00:28:11.910 --> 00:28:15.600
or what I've given here--
packages.cloud.com.

00:28:15.600 --> 00:28:18.050
Third part of policy would
be if a particular software

00:28:18.050 --> 00:28:19.050
needs any configuration.

00:28:19.050 --> 00:28:21.020
For example, in this
case, it's an MSI.

00:28:21.020 --> 00:28:22.470
I want to do it
in a certain way.

00:28:22.470 --> 00:28:23.850
And if you have other
configuration, like,

00:28:23.850 --> 00:28:25.410
I want to do like
silent install.

00:28:25.410 --> 00:28:28.270
So whatever configuration you
have, you can put it there.

00:28:28.270 --> 00:28:30.150
So think of, like, guest policy.

00:28:30.150 --> 00:28:32.700
Open up a notepad, or open
up any of the editors,

00:28:32.700 --> 00:28:35.160
create file, put
what package you

00:28:35.160 --> 00:28:38.700
want to install, where do you
want to get binaries from,

00:28:38.700 --> 00:28:40.100
put any configuration.

00:28:40.100 --> 00:28:42.150
And the next step
is, boom, take that

00:28:42.150 --> 00:28:43.405
and apply to a bunch of VMs.

00:28:46.030 --> 00:28:48.040
Now let me walk you
through how that

00:28:48.040 --> 00:28:49.960
happens in Pantheon console.

00:28:49.960 --> 00:28:51.800
This is Google Cloud console.

00:28:51.800 --> 00:28:53.775
So in this configuration
management demo,

00:28:53.775 --> 00:28:56.150
just like patch management,
I'll talk about three things.

00:28:56.150 --> 00:28:59.290
One is how do you onboard out
of the configuration management,

00:28:59.290 --> 00:29:02.530
how to use the service, and
next is how do you get this--

00:29:02.530 --> 00:29:04.317
creating a policy,
applying the policy.

00:29:04.317 --> 00:29:06.400
I'll show you some of the
key cloud commands, then

00:29:06.400 --> 00:29:08.290
assignment.

00:29:08.290 --> 00:29:10.660
The first thing is onboarding.

00:29:10.660 --> 00:29:14.020
So if you have onboarded the
patch management service, what

00:29:14.020 --> 00:29:19.363
I've showed earlier, that will
enable all our OS config APIs.

00:29:19.363 --> 00:29:20.530
So you're all taken care of.

00:29:20.530 --> 00:29:21.720
And you'll have everything done.

00:29:21.720 --> 00:29:22.870
You'll also have the
G-cloud commands.

00:29:22.870 --> 00:29:24.250
You can start using it.

00:29:24.250 --> 00:29:26.260
When we launched the
config management

00:29:26.260 --> 00:29:28.970
beta and the first half of
the year, it's API only.

00:29:28.970 --> 00:29:31.870
So we are building the UI,
the user experience there.

00:29:31.870 --> 00:29:33.490
So the API is already enabled.

00:29:33.490 --> 00:29:36.250
But in a scenario
where if you want

00:29:36.250 --> 00:29:39.070
to use only config
management-- so

00:29:39.070 --> 00:29:44.440
go to the same compute engine,
or you can go to API services.

00:29:44.440 --> 00:29:47.920
And just type OS config API.

00:29:47.920 --> 00:29:49.795
And you'll see a screen
just like this, where

00:29:49.795 --> 00:29:51.020
there will be an Enable button.

00:29:51.020 --> 00:29:52.030
In this case, I've
already enabled,

00:29:52.030 --> 00:29:53.580
so I'm showing
Disable button there.

00:29:53.580 --> 00:29:56.440
So once you enable
it, it's the same API

00:29:56.440 --> 00:29:59.132
which powers both patch
management, configuration

00:29:59.132 --> 00:30:00.840
management, other
services, [INAUDIBLE]..

00:30:00.840 --> 00:30:03.250
But links to think of
this as a common API layer

00:30:03.250 --> 00:30:05.620
where you can access that.

00:30:05.620 --> 00:30:07.905
That's the first part of
creating the configuration

00:30:07.905 --> 00:30:08.550
part.

00:30:08.550 --> 00:30:10.680
Second step as I talked
about last lecture,

00:30:10.680 --> 00:30:12.180
first thing is about
how do I create

00:30:12.180 --> 00:30:16.080
my guest policy, which is my
desired configuration there.

00:30:16.080 --> 00:30:18.690
So just start with any editor,
whatever you have, and start

00:30:18.690 --> 00:30:20.250
drafting your AML file--

00:30:20.250 --> 00:30:21.750
what package do you
want to install,

00:30:21.750 --> 00:30:23.385
what configuration do
you want, where do you

00:30:23.385 --> 00:30:24.510
get the repo manager there.

00:30:24.510 --> 00:30:27.580
Or you can take any
from other examples.

00:30:27.580 --> 00:30:31.283
So if you go to our
configuration recourse link--

00:30:31.283 --> 00:30:32.700
and I've added all
of these things

00:30:32.700 --> 00:30:34.440
in the later parts of the
slide, so you can actually

00:30:34.440 --> 00:30:35.480
look at that stuff.

00:30:35.480 --> 00:30:37.452
But if you go to
Google Compute Engine

00:30:37.452 --> 00:30:39.660
and take configuration
management, setting up config,

00:30:39.660 --> 00:30:41.800
you can see all
those examples here.

00:30:41.800 --> 00:30:43.710
In this we have provided
a lot of examples,

00:30:43.710 --> 00:30:46.440
like, how to install a
stack driver agent, how

00:30:46.440 --> 00:30:48.200
to install other things there.

00:30:48.200 --> 00:30:49.950
So there's a lot of
examples you can see--

00:30:49.950 --> 00:30:52.117
what state do you want, how
do you do an assignment,

00:30:52.117 --> 00:30:53.665
where you want to keep it--

00:30:53.665 --> 00:30:54.540
it's lot of examples.

00:30:54.540 --> 00:30:57.198
Then it talks about how
to create a guest policy.

00:30:57.198 --> 00:30:58.740
So there is a simple
G-Cloud command,

00:30:58.740 --> 00:31:00.690
what we use to go
create a policy,

00:31:00.690 --> 00:31:02.770
so that it brings
into the system.

00:31:02.770 --> 00:31:05.720
So for my demo, in
the interest of time,

00:31:05.720 --> 00:31:08.640
I've already created the
policies and everything

00:31:08.640 --> 00:31:10.030
and got to the system.

